Chronic Migraine (Neuroinflammatory)
Complex Fatigue Entities
High-frequency refractory headaches

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-007
Reason for Visit: Urgent management of severe, unremitting migraine with profound neurocognitive features preventing oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a 48-hour refractory headache with severe photophobia and persistent vomiting. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out secondary intracranial pathology (e.g., meningitis, hemorrhage) and to administer parenteral rescue therapy to abort the attack and prevent severe dehydration prior to regular clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head feels like it's going to explode, the light is burning my eyes, and I can't stop vomiting.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a high-frequency refractory headache evolving over 48 hours. The episode began with a classic aura, progressing to intense throbbing unilateral temporal pain. Currently exhibiting profound photophobia, cognitive slowing, and symptoms consistent with cortical spreading depression. Nausea is severe, resulting in three episodes of emesis today, preventing oral hydration or medication retention.
Functional Status: Bedbound, requiring a dark, silent room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of severe dehydration; partner highly distressed by patient's cognitive slowing and lethargy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Lethargic, eyes tightly closed, distressed by ambient light and sound. Mild clinical dehydration.
Physical Exam: High-frequency refractory headaches evident. Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves. No nuchal rigidity; Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs negative. Pupils equal and reactive to light. Fundoscopy normal without papilledema. Tachycardia present, likely secondary to pain and volume depletion.
Environmental Observations: Room is heavily darkened. Emesis basin next to bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral Sumatriptan 50mg taken 24 hours ago, vomited immediately.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Migraine (Neuroinflammatory) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Subarachnoid hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual crescendo onset rather than a sudden "thunderclap" presentation.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal neck flexion, and clear sensorium despite cognitive slowing from pain.

Tension headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Unilateral throbbing nature, presence of aura, vomiting, and severe photophobia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's unremitting migraine with prominent autonomic features (vomiting, tachycardia) and failure of oral rescue therapy required immediate after-hours intervention. In-home evaluation definitively excluded red-flag intracranial emergencies. Prompt administration of parenteral antiemetics and acute migraine-abortive therapies was necessary to break the neuroinflammatory cycle, restore oral intake capability, and prevent an otherwise inevitable emergency department admission for intravenous hydration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Prochlorperazine 12.5mg IM and Ketorolac 30mg IM to abort the migraine cycle and manage nausea. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, Magnesium, and Vitamin B2/B6. Recommended future routine labs to exclude underlying inflammatory markers.
Positive Behavioral Change: By maintaining a strict, consistent sleep schedule and hydration routine, you will stabilize your brain's electrical environment, actively reducing the frequency of cortical spreading depression and rewarding yourself with more headache-free days.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None acutely required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to optimize prophylactic therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via 000 if new focal neurological signs develop (e.g., unilateral weakness, aphasia), worst headache of life occurs suddenly, or fever spikes above 38.5 C.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on clinical patterns; any change in headache character warrants urgent neuroimaging.
Clinical Course: Migraine should abort within 2-4 hours post-injection. The post-drome phase (fatigue, mild cognitive slowing) will persist for 24-48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately if you suddenly cannot speak clearly, or if one side of your body goes completely numb.

